{"items":[
{"label":"1B","direction":"against","claim":"Natera's unit costs per test have barely moved over seven quarters, and its rising cost per test is explained almost entirely by the shift toward oncology tests. That shows no visible pass-through of falling Illumina prices at Illumina's largest NIPT/MRD customer.","detail":"Cost of product revenue divided by tests processed gives ~$216 (Q3'24), ~$216 (Q1'25), $234 (Q2'25), $232 (Q3'25), ~$237 (Q4'25), $242 (Q1'26) and $255 (Q2'26). A two-segment fit with fixed unit costs (solved on Q2'25 and Q2'26) gives ~$500 per oncology test and ~$158 per non-oncology test. With no change in unit costs, that fit reproduces Q3'24, Q1'25, Q3'25, Q4'25 and Q1'26 within +1% to +4%. Example for Q1'26: 754.7K×$158 + 258.9K×$500 = $248.7M vs $245.2M actual. So unit costs fell at most ~2–4% over two years, a period in which Illumina's per-Gb pricing fell mid-teens a year. Either Natera reinvests the savings in depth or its Illumina price is not falling much.","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1604821/000162828026032101/ntra-20260331xex991.htm","date":"2024-11 to 2026-08 (Q3'24–Q2'26 releases)","source_name":"Natera 8-K Ex. 99.1 earnings releases (Q3'25, Q4'25, Q1'26, Q2'26)","source_type":"filing","quote":"continued progress in reducing cost of revenues associated with tests processed","strength":"medium","why_new":"The known file has only the two-point $255 vs $234 cost per test. This adds an 8-quarter series and a mix decomposition showing flat unit costs."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"Clinical MRD blood draws make up 94–96% of Natera's oncology tests, so its headline oncology growth is almost all low-Gb Signatera draws. Non-MRD oncology tests (tissue/CGP/other) are only ~6–14K a quarter.","detail":"Oncology tests minus clinical MRD units: Q3'24 137.1K−130K=7.1K; Q4'24 150.8K−145K=5.8K; Q1'25 167.7K−161K=6.7K; Q2'25 188.8K−181K=7.8K; Q3'25 211.0K−202K=9.0K; Q4'25 233.3K−225K=8.3K; Q1'26 258.9K−249K=9.9K; Q2'26 296.7K−283K=13.7K. The +57% oncology growth is therefore MRD draws at ~0.16–0.24 Gb each (known per-draw Gb), not Gb-heavy CGP. On that basis the record +34K q/q MRD add in Q2'26 is only ~5–8 TB of draw sequencing a quarter (34K × 0.16–0.24 Gb).","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001604821/000160482126000015/ntra-20260630xex992.htm","date":"2026-08-06","source_name":"Natera Q2'26 earnings deck (8-K Ex. 99.2) and Ex. 99.1 releases","source_type":"filing","quote":"Clinical MRD oncology units (Signatera plus Latitude)","strength":"medium","why_new":"The known file has oncology test growth and MRD totals but not the MRD share of oncology tests or the tiny non-MRD residual series."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"Natera's clinical MRD unit growth has held at +52–56% y/y for six straight quarters with no deceleration, and sequential adds keep rising.","detail":"Clinical MRD units (Signatera + Latitude) per the Q1'26/Q2'26 decks: 106K, 118K, 130K, 145K (2024); 161K, 181K, 202K, 225K (2025); 249K, 283K (H1'26). Y/y growth: Q1'25 +51.9%, Q2'25 +53.4%, Q3'25 +55.4%, Q4'25 +55.2%, Q1'26 +54.7%, Q2'26 +56.4%. Q/q adds: +12, +12, +15, +16, +20, +21, +23, +24, +34K. Management notes Q2 was partly weather catch-up, but even ex-Q2 the y/y rate is not slowing.","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001604821/000162828026032101/ntra-20260331xex992.htm","date":"2026-05-07 / 2026-08-06","source_name":"Natera Q1'26 and Q2'26 earnings decks (8-K Ex. 99.2)","source_type":"filing","quote":"Fastest unit growth quarter at ~24K volume growth","strength":"medium","why_new":"The known file has the Q2'26 +56% point and the q/q adds (F176). The full 10-quarter level series and the flat y/y growth trend are new."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"Natera's new single-gene NIPT, Fetal Focus, was nearing ~200K orders a year by Q1'26. That is a new prenatal sequencing product that partly offsets NIPT maturation.","detail":"The Q1'26 deck says Fetal Focus is nearing an annualized pace of ~200K orders (~50K a quarter), after a 2026 launch milestone in the Q4'25 deck (21-gene single-gene NIPT). For scale, ~50K a quarter is ~7% of Natera's ~747K non-oncology tests in Q2'26. A gene-panel cfDNA test likely carries more Gb than SNP-based aneuploidy NIPT (our inference, undisclosed). Women's health had a 'record quarter… second fastest growth since 2019' (Q1'26). Management cites Fetal Focus as a driver of women's-health share gains.","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001604821/000162828026032101/ntra-20260331xex992.htm","date":"2026-05-07","source_name":"Natera Q1'26 earnings deck (8-K Ex. 99.2)","source_type":"filing","quote":"Record quarter for Women's Health, second fastest growth since 2019","strength":"medium","why_new":"Fetal Focus order volume is not in the known file or the found list."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"Texas building records show Natera is building a dedicated 'Natera Sequencing Core' in Austin, due by 31 Dec 2026, on top of ~$23M of other 2025–26 Austin lab build-outs. Large new sequencing capacity at Illumina's biggest NIPT/MRD customer comes online for 2027.","detail":"TDLR TABS2026019294, 'Natera Sequencing Core': $6.0M, 12,048 sq ft, lab remodel plus an extra make-up air unit, 13011 McCallen Pass Bldg A, registered 4 May 2026, start 8 Jun 2026, completion 31 Dec 2026. Earlier projects: TABS2025012493 'Pista – Level 3 South', $9.9M, 24,000 sq ft of new lab, Apr–Dec 2025; 'Enzo' (TABS2025018660), $9.4M, office/lab/warehouse; 'Daytona' (TABS2026008903), $4.0M, 43,960 sq ft office→lab, 2026 (the last figure is from a third-party tracker snippet). The 'largest sequencing capacity in the world' claim (May 2026) fits a ~12K sq ft room, so the core houses dozens of high-throughput boxes, not hundreds (our inference). Model unnamed.","url":"https://www.tdlr.texas.gov/TABS/Projects/TABS2026019294","date":"2026-05-04","source_name":"Texas Department of Licensing and Regulation, TABS project registry","source_type":"procurement","quote":"Partial demolition and remodel of existing interior space for lab use","strength":"medium","why_new":"The known file has only the May 2026 Austin press release with no size, cost or timing. These permit records add dollars, square footage and a Dec 2026 completion date."},
{"label":"1B","direction":"supports","claim":"Natera says its newest products (Signatera Genome, Fetal Focus, Latitude) are not yet cost-optimized and that it has a COGS-reduction roadmap. That is the Gb-heavy launch where it will push hardest on sequencing input costs.","detail":"On the Q2'26 call (6 Aug 2026), management blamed the slight COGS rise on volume from recently launched products not yet optimized for cost. It said it leaves 'substantial room' for COGS improvement as volume scales and is working through that roadmap. The CFO said cost projects pay off over the next 12–18 months (GuruFocus summary). In Q1'26, ~2 pts of 'transient' gross-margin impact included COGS tied to volume acceleration (a higher receive-to-report ratio). Signatera Genome is the ~10–15x Gb-per-draw product (known), so its cost-down is where Illumina's per-Gb price is most exposed.","url":"https://www.gurufocus.com/news/9015362/natera-inc-ntra-q2-2026-earnings-call-highlights-record-volumes-and-raised-guidance-signal-strong-momentum","date":"2026-08-06","source_name":"GuruFocus Q2'26 call summary; Natera Q1'26 deck","source_type":"earnings_call","quote":"not yet optimized for cost efficiency","strength":"weak","why_new":"The known file has Natera's cost per test but not management's naming of Signatera Genome and Fetal Focus as unoptimized-COGS products with a 12–18-month cost roadmap."},
{"label":"1B","direction":"supports","claim":"Natera's Q2'26 10-Q re-filed its 2013–2016 Illumina supply-agreement chain (Ex. 10.1–10.4). The re-filed exhibits show Illumina's grants are non-exclusive and that NIPT test fees count toward Natera's discount tiers.","detail":"Ex. 10.1 is the 2013 Supply Agreement (TG Consumables), which includes a 4-month termination-for-convenience right for Natera. Ex. 10.2 (Second Amendment, Sep 2015) defines 'Consumable Spend' as trailing-12-month Illumina invoices plus NIPT Test Fees, with discounts capped at a redacted top tier. Ex. 10.3 (Third Amendment, Jun 2016) adds clinical oncology testing (including LDTs) and organ-transplant monitoring as non-exclusive fields, with a redacted revenue share on Natera IVD kits and Subject Tests. It also ran 10 years (to ~Jun 2026), with Illumina able to end NIPT/clinical rights on 2 years' notice after year 5. The supply agreement now runs to Aug 2033 (known), so these terms were extended. Filing the old chain in Aug 2026 may reflect an SEC review or a new amendment; unverified.","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1604821/000162828026054525/ntra-20260630xex103.htm","date":"2026-08-07","source_name":"Natera 10-Q Q2'26, Exhibits 10.1–10.3","source_type":"filing","quote":"the non-exclusive, non–transferable","strength":"weak","why_new":"F238 covers only Ex. 10.4. The re-filing of Ex. 10.1–10.3, the 2016 oncology/transplant field grant and the 10-year term are new."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"On per-test Gb, Natera's MRD draws are probably a fraction of its NIPT Gb, so even +56% MRD unit growth adds little sequencing volume unless Signatera Genome scales.","detail":"Q2'26 draw-level Gb: 283K × 0.16–0.24 Gb ≈ 45–68 TB. Non-oncology tests (1,043.9K − 296.7K = 747.2K) × 1–2 Gb ≈ 0.75–1.5 PB. Draw Gb is therefore ~3–9% of non-oncology Gb before one-off tumor exomes; if exomes are 80–90% of MRD Gb per patient-year (known), total MRD Gb is ~0.2–0.7 PB, ~15–90% of NIPT Gb. Caveat: Panorama is a targeted SNP-amplicon assay whose reads per sample are undisclosed, and the May 2026 'SNP-informed deep sequencing' upgrade may raise NIPT Gb per test.","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001604821/000160482126000015/ntra-20260630xex991.htm","date":"2026-08-06","source_name":"Natera Q2'26 release (tests processed); our calc using known per-test Gb","source_type":"filing","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"The known file gives per-test Gb but has no Natera-specific MRD-vs-NIPT Gb split."}
